WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.919
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.919 --> 00:00:11.480
Today, we are going to see Microsoft’s AI
looking at a lot of people who don’t exist,

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:17.900
and then, we will see that these virtual people
can teach it something about real people.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:23.060
Now, through the power of computer graphics
algorithms, we are able to create virtual

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:28.550
worlds, and of course, within those virtual
worlds, virtual humans too.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.220
So, here is a wacky idea.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:36.920
If we have all this virtual data, why not
use these instead of real photos to train

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.379
a new AI to do useful things with them?

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:43.020
Hmm…wait a second.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.980
Maybe this idea is not so wacky after all.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:52.659
Especially because we can generate as many
of these virtual humans as we wish, and all

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:55.879
this data is perfectly annotated.

00:00:55.879 --> 00:01:01.600
The location and shape of the eyebrows is
known, even when they are occluded, and we

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.510
know the depth and geometry of every single
hair strand of the beard.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:12.880
If done well, there will be no issues about
the identity of the subjects, or the distribution

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:13.970
of the data.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:19.450
Also, we are not limited by our wardrobe or
the environments we have access to.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.260
In this virtual world, we can do anything
we wish.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.260
So good!

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:29.570
But of course, here is the ultimate question
that decides the fate of this project.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.170
And that question is: does this work?

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.560
What is all this good for?

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.170
And the crazy thing is that Microsoft’s
previous AI technique could now identify facial

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:46.900
landmarks of real people, but, it has never
seen a real person before.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.270
How cool is that!

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:56.330
But, this is a previous work, and now, a new
paper has emerged, and in this one, scientists

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:01.950
at Microsoft said, how about more than 10
times more landmarks?

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:07.430
Yes, this new paper promises no less than
700.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.500
When I saw this, I thought - are you kidding?

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.400
Are we going 10x just one more paper down
the line?

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.030
Well, I will believe it when I see it.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:22.180
Let’s see a different previous technique
from just two years ago.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.510
You see that we have temporal consistency
issues, in other words, there is plenty of

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:33.489
flickering going on here, and there is one
more problem: these facial expressions are

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:35.849
really giving it a hard time.

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:39.709
Can we really expect any improvement over
these two years?

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:46.750
Well, hold on to your papers and let’s have
a look at the new method and see for ourselves.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:47.750
Look at that!

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:53.560
It not only tracks a ton more landmarks, but
the consistency of the results has improved

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.090
a ton as well.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:03:02.370
So, it both solves a harder problem, and it
also does it better than the previous technique.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.540
Wow!

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.209
And all this just one more paper down the
line.

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:08.779
My goodness.

00:03:08.779 --> 00:03:10.349
I love it!

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:15.670
I feel like this new method is the first that
could even track Jim Carrey himself.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.379
And, we are not done yet!

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:21.459
Not even close - it gets even better!

00:03:21.459 --> 00:03:27.079
I was wondering if it still works in the presence
of occlusions, for instance, whenever the

00:03:27.079 --> 00:03:31.939
face is covered by hair or clothing, or a
flower.

00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:35.019
And, let’s see.

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:37.349
It still works amazingly well!

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:39.659
What about the colors?

00:03:39.659 --> 00:03:45.419
That is the other really cool thing - it can,
for instance, tell us how confident it is

00:03:45.419 --> 00:03:46.419
in these predictions.

00:03:46.419 --> 00:03:51.290
Green means confident, red means that the
AI has to do more guesswork, often because

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.909
of these occlusions.

00:03:52.909 --> 00:03:57.669
My other favorite thing is that this is still
trained with synthetic data.

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:01.370
In fact, it is key to its success.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:06.219
This is one of those success stories where
training an AI in a simulated world can be

00:04:06.219 --> 00:04:10.269
brought into the real world, and it still
works spectacularly.

00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:17.079
There is a lot of factors at play here, so
let’s send out a huge thank you to computer

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:20.699
graphics researchers as well for making this
happen.

00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:26.080
These virtual characters could not be rendered
and animated in real time without decades

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.150
of incredible graphics research works.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.410
Thank you!

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:35.660
And now comes the ultimate question: how much
do we have to wait for these results?

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.230
This is incredibly important.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:39.230
Why?

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:44.410
Well, here is a previous technique that was
amazing at tracking our hand movements.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.780
Do you see these gloves?

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:47.780
Yes?

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.229
Well, those are not gloves.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:54.930
This is how a previous method understands
our hand motions, which is to say, that it

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.250
can reconstruct them nearly perfectly.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:04:59.669
Stunning work.

00:04:59.669 --> 00:05:05.580
However, these are typically used in virtual
worlds, and we had to wait for nearly an hour

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.150
for such a reconstruction to happen.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.840
Do we have the same situation here?

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:17.110
You know, 10x better results in facial landmark
detection, so what is the price that we have

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:18.419
to pay for this?

00:05:18.419 --> 00:05:20.240
One hour of waiting again?

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.060
Well, not at all!

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:27.699
If you have been holding on to your papers,
now, squeeze that paper, because it is not

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:33.000
only real time, it is more than twice as fast
as real time.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.900
It can churn out 150 frames per second and
it doesn’t even require your graphics card,

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.500
it runs on your processor.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.050
That is incredible.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.979
Here is one more comparison against the competitors.

00:05:47.979 --> 00:05:53.409
For instance, Apple’s ARKit runs on their
own iPhones, and thus, they can make use of

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:56.110
the additional depth information.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:57.689
That is a goldmine of information.

00:05:57.689 --> 00:06:04.860
But, this new technique doesn’t, it just
takes color data, that is so much harder,

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.759
but in return, it will run on any phone.

00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:12.819
Can these results compete with Apple’s solution
with less data?

00:06:12.819 --> 00:06:15.360
Let’s have a look.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.699
My goodness, I love it.

00:06:17.699 --> 00:06:22.110
The results seem at the very least comparably
good.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:26.110
That is, once again, amazing progress in just
one paper.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:27.110
So cool!

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:32.169
Also, what I am really excited about is that
variants of this technique may also be able

00:06:32.169 --> 00:06:35.900
to improve the fidelity of these DeepFake
videos out there.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:41.250
For instance, here is an example of me becoming
a bunch of characters from Game of Thrones,

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:47.930
this previous work was incredible because
it could even track where I was looking.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:53.879
Imagine a new generation of these tools that
is able to track even more facial landmarks,

00:06:53.879 --> 00:06:59.770
and democratize creating movies, games and
all kinds of virtual worlds.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:05.259
Yes, with some of these techniques, we can
even become a painting or a virtual character

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:10.210
as well, and even the movement of our nostrils
would be transferred.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:11.830
What a time to be alive!

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:14.480
So, does this get your mind going?

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.380
What would you use this for?

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:19.220
Let me know in the comments below!

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:51.930
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see 

00:07:51.930 --> 00:08:22.489
you next time!

